# Lesson 3, new exercise: count losers in a partscore; draw trumps even though you must lose the ace.
title: Five losers in 2♠
id: L03-04
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.3, 3.4
student: N
board: 36
dealer: W
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  KQJT5  A84    K63    72
  E  A9     QJT9   972    KJ94
  S  862    K52    QJ54   Q63
  W  743    763    AT8    AT85

auction:
  W Pass
  N 1S*    = 13+ points, at least four spades
     not *: 13 high-card points and five spades: what does the lesson open?
  E Pass
  S 2S     = 6-9 points and spade support
  W Pass
  N Pass*  = No game: at most 23
     not *: Partner showed 6-9. Can 14 plus 6-9 reach 25?
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ and partner raised to 2♠, showing 6-9 points. Your side
  can't have 25, so you stopped in a partscore. You are declarer, because
  you bid spades first, and so East will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 5
  losers: 5
  spades: 1 = The ace. Lose it early, then draw trumps.
  hearts: 1 = The third heart.
  diamonds: 1 = The ace. Force it out, and the rest are winners.
  clubs: 2 = The ace and king.
  remind: In 2♠ you need 8 tricks, so you can afford five losers. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand.
play:
  1   E HQ  S H2  W H3  N HA
      note: Count your losers: the ♠A, one heart, the ♦A and two clubs. Five, exactly what 2♠ can afford. Keep dummy's ♥K for later.
  2   N SK  E SA  S S2  W S3
      note: Draw trumps, even though you must lose the ace. The ♠A is a loser either way, so lose it now, while you still control every other suit.
  3   E HJ  S HK  W H6  N H4
  4   S S6  W S4  N SQ  E S9
      note: Keep drawing trumps. Two are still out.
  5   N SJ  E D2  S S8  W S7
      note: The last trump. Now the opponents can't ruff your winners.
  6   N D3  E D7  S DQ  W DA
      note: Now force out the ♦A. Your king and dummy's jack will be winners.
  7   W H7  N H8  E HT  S H5
      note: West returns partner's hearts. Your third heart was always a loser.
  8   E C4  S C3  W CA  N C2
  9   W C5  N C7  E CK  S C6
      note: That's five tricks for the defense, all that you counted.
  10  E H9  S D4  W C8  N S5
      note: Ruff, and take the rest.
  11  N DK  E D9  S D5  W D8
      note: Your diamonds are all winners now.
  12  N D6  E C9  S DJ  W DT
      note: Over to dummy's ♦J.
  13  S CQ  W CT  N ST  E CJ
      note: 8 tricks: 2♠ made.

play recap:
  The plan: count five losers, exactly what 2♠ can afford, then draw trumps
  and force out the ♦A.

  You lost the lead four times, and each time it did you no harm. Losing
  the tricks you counted as losers is part of the plan.
